3
00:00:08.257 --> 00:00:15.576
Unit thirteen: Science Communication. In this unit, you will discuss how science is explained to the public.

4
00:00:16.583 --> 00:00:18.397
scientific consensus.

5
00:00:19.097 --> 00:00:24.568
scientific consensus means general agreement among scientists based on evidence.

6
00:00:24.868 --> 00:00:28.330
There is a strong scientific consensus on climate change.

7
00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:32.519
Your turn. Say: scientific consensus.

8
00:00:35.219 --> 00:00:37.091
scientific consensus.

9
00:00:38.083 --> 00:00:39.537
misinterpretation.

10
00:00:40.237 --> 00:00:43.827
misinterpretation means understanding information wrongly.

11
00:00:44.127 --> 00:00:47.555
Headlines often lead to misinterpretation of research.

12
00:00:48.255 --> 00:00:51.673
Your turn. Say: misinterpretation.

13
00:00:54.373 --> 00:00:55.793
misinterpretation.

14
00:00:56.783 --> 00:00:58.330
popular science.

15
00:00:59.030 --> 00:01:02.887
popular science means science explained for a general audience.

16
00:01:03.187 --> 00:01:06.592
Popular science books made physics exciting for me.

17
00:01:07.292 --> 00:01:10.478
Your turn. Say: popular science.

18
00:01:13.178 --> 00:01:14.527
popular science.

19
00:01:15.517 --> 00:01:16.390
jargon.

20
00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:22.306
jargon means special words used by experts that others find hard to understand.

21
00:01:22.606 --> 00:01:24.710
The report was full of medical jargon.

22
00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:28.050
Your turn. Say: jargon.

23
00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:31.519
jargon.

24
00:01:32.517 --> 00:01:34.110
data visualisation.

25
00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:39.642
data visualisation means showing information as charts, maps or graphics.

26
00:01:39.942 --> 00:01:43.103
Good data visualisation makes complex trends clear.

27
00:01:43.803 --> 00:01:46.815
Your turn. Say: data visualisation.

28
00:01:49.515 --> 00:01:51.027
data visualisation.

29
00:01:52.017 --> 00:01:53.169
uncertainty.

30
00:01:53.869 --> 00:01:59.700
uncertainty means not being completely sure; the range of possible results.

31
00:01:60.000 --> 00:02:03.729
Scientists always report the uncertainty in their results.

32
00:02:04.429 --> 00:02:07.267
Your turn. Say: uncertainty.

33
00:02:09.967 --> 00:02:10.945
uncertainty.

34
00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:13.729
replication crisis.

35
00:02:14.429 --> 00:02:19.726
replication crisis means the problem that many studies cannot be repeated with the same results.

36
00:02:20.026 --> 00:02:23.047
The replication crisis has shaken psychology.

37
00:02:23.747 --> 00:02:27.409
Your turn. Say: replication crisis.

38
00:02:30.109 --> 00:02:31.842
replication crisis.

39
00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:34.072
public trust.

40
00:02:34.772 --> 00:02:39.639
public trust means the confidence people have in institutions or experts.

41
00:02:39.939 --> 00:02:43.320
Clear communication helps build public trust in science.

42
00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:47.241
Your turn. Say: public trust.

43
00:02:49.941 --> 00:02:51.058
public trust.

44
00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:54.177
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:54.477 --> 00:02:58.427
Rithy, why do so many people distrust science these days?

46
00:02:59.717 --> 00:03:05.318
Jargon, mainly. Scientists often speak in a language nobody else understands.

47
00:03:06.617 --> 00:03:11.254
That's part of it. Uncertainty, too, is often misunderstood.

48
00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:16.479
Yes. When a study says 'probably', the media report 'definitely'.

49
00:03:17.783 --> 00:03:22.746
Exactly. Much more damaging, though, is the replication crisis.

50
00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:27.689
Really? I thought that only affected a few fields.

51
00:03:28.983 --> 00:03:34.956
It has affected several. Still, the scientific consensus on major issues remains strong.

52
00:03:36.250 --> 00:03:41.233
What we need, then, is better popular science and clearer data visualisation.

53
00:03:42.517 --> 00:03:45.491
Grammar focus: Fronting for emphasis.

54
00:03:45.791 --> 00:03:49.637
Fronting means moving a part of the sentence to the front for emphasis.

55
00:03:49.937 --> 00:03:56.467
Uncertainty, too, is often misunderstood. Much more damaging is the replication crisis.

56
00:03:56.767 --> 00:04:01.089
After a fronted adjective phrase, the verb comes before the subject.

57
00:04:01.389 --> 00:04:08.113
Fronting highlights contrast or new information; it is common in persuasive speech and writing.

58
00:04:09.117 --> 00:04:14.892
Now read and listen. Lost in Translation: Science and the Public.

59
00:04:15.192 --> 00:04:20.477
Science shapes decisions about health, energy and the environment.

60
00:04:21.783 --> 00:04:27.452
Yet many people find scientific information confusing or even suspicious.

61
00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:33.838
Part of the problem is jargon, which excludes non-experts from the conversation.

62
00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:39.402
Equally important is the way uncertainty is communicated.

63
00:04:40.717 --> 00:04:46.118
Scientists use words like 'likely' carefully, but headlines often turn them into certainties.

64
00:04:47.417 --> 00:04:52.249
The replication crisis has also given critics reasons to doubt research.

65
00:04:53.550 --> 00:05:00.426
Clear data visualisation and honest explanations of uncertainty can rebuild public trust.

66
00:05:01.717 --> 00:05:04.726
Good science, in short, needs good storytellers.

67
00:05:06.017 --> 00:05:08.692
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:08.992 --> 00:05:12.106
Special words used by experts are blank.

69
00:05:12.406 --> 00:05:16.849
A, jargon. B, uncertainty. C, consensus.

70
00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:23.105
The answer is A: jargon.

71
00:05:24.617 --> 00:05:26.419
Which sentence uses fronting?

72
00:05:26.719 --> 00:05:33.600
A, The results were surprising.. B, Surprising were the results.. C, The results surprised us..

73
00:05:37.583 --> 00:05:40.686
The answer is B: Surprising were the results..

74
00:05:42.183 --> 00:05:45.205
Much more important blank the quality of the data.

75
00:05:45.505 --> 00:05:48.694
A, is. B, are. C, be.

76
00:05:52.683 --> 00:05:54.799
The answer is A: is.

77
00:05:56.283 --> 00:05:59.827
The problem that many studies cannot be repeated is the blank.

78
00:06:00.127 --> 00:06:05.383
A, replication crisis. B, public trust. C, popular science.

79
00:06:09.383 --> 00:06:12.730
The answer is A: replication crisis.

80
00:06:14.217 --> 00:06:17.772
According to the text, what can rebuild public trust?

81
00:06:18.072 --> 00:06:24.930
A, more jargon. B, clear data visualisation and honesty. C, fewer studies.

82
00:06:28.917 --> 00:06:33.029
The answer is B: clear data visualisation and honesty.

83
00:06:34.517 --> 00:06:40.884
Now it's your turn. Part one: How can scientists communicate more effectively with the public?

84
00:06:46.184 --> 00:06:52.375
Part two: Use fronting at least once: propose two strategies and give an example.

85
00:06:58.675 --> 00:07:12.348
Most important of all is avoiding jargon. Simple language and clear visuals would help enormously. During the pandemic, for example, simple charts explained the spread of the virus better than long reports.

86
00:07:12.648 --> 00:07:15.007
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:16.017 --> 00:07:21.026
Great work today! You learned eight new words and fronting for emphasis.

88
00:07:21.326 --> 00:07:25.464
Today's value is honesty. Always tell the truth.

89
00:07:25.764 --> 00:07:28.660
See you in Unit fourteen. Goodbye!

90
00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:31.648
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

